'''Nungali''', or (with a different prefix) '''Yilngali''', is an [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian language]] which is believed to be [[language death|extinct]]. It was spoken in the [[Northern Territory]] of [[Australia]], around the [[Daly River, Northern Territory|upper Daly River]].<ref name="ethnologue">[http://www.ethnologue.com/show_language.asp?code=nug Ethnologue]</ref> Its closest relative is the [[Jaminjung language]].

== Grammar ==

=== Cases ===
It is the only [[Yirram languages|Yirram language]] which has retained the original [[noun class|four-class system]] in [[Nominal (linguistics)|nominals]]. The four classes are [[grammatical gender|masculine]], [[grammatical gender|feminine]], [[grammatical gender|neuter]] and plants, and each of the classes have separate [[prefix]]es expressing the [[absolutive case]], [[locative case|locative]] or [[ergative case]], and the [[dative case]].<ref name="patrick_mcconvell_2009">{{Citation | last1 = McConvell | first1 = Patrick | author1-link = Patrick McConvell | editor1-last = Koch | editor1-first = Harold | editor1-link = Harold Koch | editor2-last = Hercus | editor2-first = Luise | editor2-link = Luise Hercus | title = Aboriginal Placenames: Naming and re-naming the Australian landscape | chapter = 'Where the spear sticks up' - The variety of locatives in placenames in the Victoria River District, Northern Territory | year = 2009 | pages = 359–402 | publisher = [[Australian National University|ANU E-Press]] | isbn = 978-1-921666-08-7}}</ref>

